// Act Six, "By Your Grace", the second and third legs (WRITE-20B: new in round twenty; briefs/WRITE-20.md, briefs/ROUND-20.md,
// and the design, briefs/out/edo-design.md, puzzles #25 to #30). The hills north of Osaka (the cedar path, the storehouse,
// the room under it) and Himeji (the castle approach, the keep, the top floor); the walk card and the step. WRITE-20A writes
// the first leg (edo-a.en.js); MERGE-20 joins the two into edo.en.js.
//   "line.id": ["who", "What they say."]
// Scene-shorts: cedars, store (the storehouse), chapel (the room), castle, keep, top. A look is edo.<short>.<thing>.<who>,
// a use edo.<short>.<thing>.use.<who>, a use with a carried thing edo.<short>.<thing>.with.<item>.<who>; a look that
// changes with the story has a state word before the who (edo.chapel.altar.open.mom). The talks are trees
// (js/content/talk/edo-b.js); an option is a line here, said by the one who picks it. S/work-20-writeB/ids.md says when
// every line is said.
//
// THE RULES OF THE ACT, as the lines keep them:
//   - Nobody in the family lies: every answer any of them gives an official is true, and the comedy is in exactly true
//     answers ("The sea." "Most things come from the sea, eventually.").
//   - Scripture inside quotation marks is the King James Version exactly: Matthew 6:9, its prayer (edo.chapel.pray.lilsis:
//     the verse begins "After this manner therefore pray ye:", so the quotation opens with "..."), and John 1:5 whole
//     (edo.chapel.verse.mom, said twice, and edo.chapel.verse.sei, the grandmother saying it after her). The hidden family's
//     own prayer is written only as its first three words, "Ten ni mashimasu" (edo.chapel.pray.sei), which Big Sister
//     glosses without quotation marks. Mom's prayer at the door (edo.step.pray.*) and Little Sister's are their own words.
//   - Japanese words are few, of the period, and glossed once: "Kannon-sama" (Grandmother Sei), "Kirishitan" (Jinsuke's
//     one line about Osaka: "they took six people the magistrates called Kirishitan"), "arigatō" (Little Sister's, learned
//     at Yui in the first leg; she says it to everyone here). No "itadakimasu", no "gochisōsama"; the castle is "the castle".
//   - Big Sister's facts here are the fact files' (S/work-20-facts/facts-edo.md, S/work-20-factsB/facts-edo-2.md): no
//     priest in Japan since 1644, nearly two hundred years; the cross tile, nobody knows; a double cherry opens a week or
//     two after the single ones; the pilgrims' ladle as the badge, held out, and Ise; the keep's five roofs and six floors;
//     gun ports round, triangular and square; a bronze mirror's back showing in its light, and its slight convexity
//     making the patch bigger farther off. Everything else she says is her reading of the room.
//   - Nothing frightening: the constable is wet, sleepy and kind; the warden is afraid of a story, and the story is kind;
//     the arrests in Osaka are one line; the weapon racks are scenery ("No.").
//   - Shinnosuke's poems are five, seven and five syllables in English, the game's own: three in the keep, each more
//     frightened than the last (and a fourth, for the asking), and the last at the top, not frightened at all.
//   - The Japanese people keep whole words (Sei, Jinsuke, the guards, Shinnosuke in his poems); Isuke and Tomezō
//     contract, one sleepy, one shouting. The family contract as they do everywhere.
// WRITE-21B (round twenty-one, the history pass: briefs/out/edo2-design.md, section 10): ten lines here are changed, each
// marked "// WRITE-21B: was ..." with its old words and why: the walk is four days from the hills and its card ends at
// Akashi (edo.walk.card.2; Jinsuke's cousin, the castle road, two hints of #27's end); no named weekdays (Tomezō and a
// plasterer); Mom's prayer at the door, now that she has heard her son (edo.step.pray.1); and the hills' far line, now the
// fallback for the per-leg lines in edo-d.en.js. Every id is kept.

